product 1 and 15 of product 0.\n  - Candidate 0 orders even more, so its cuts are deeper still.\n  - Overall, candidate 4 comes out about 120 behind candidate 3 and candidate 2 about 235 behind.\n- **Candidates 1, 3 and 5 (order daily):**\n  - Candidate 5 (about 1.2× forecast) overbuys. The extra product 0 costs 11 a unit but only saves about 0.3 shortage units each, and it also adds holding cost. It ends up roughly 250 behind candidate 1.\n  - Candidate 3 orders exactly the forecast. But demand averages about 1.046× forecast, so it under-orders all the time and drifts into shortages: about 55 units short for product 0 and about 73 for product 1.\n  - Candidate 1 orders about 1.09× forecast for product 0, and for product 1 about the average demand. That cuts shortages to roughly 30 and 48 units. The extra purchases and storage cost about the same as the penalties and lost sales they prevent.\n\n**Effect of random demand.** Without a delivery schedule that adjusts, unsold stock carries over and builds up, so every surplus unit costs money too. Even so, a missed sale costs more than a surplus unit: 23 against 11 for product 0, and 17 against 8 for product 1. That argues for ordering at least the average demand, not the bare forecast, and it tips the balance to candidate 1. The risk on the other side is that candidate 1 keeps less cash in reserve on days 7–10, so a run of low demand could trigger extra cuts."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 355, "cache_read_input_tokens": 5807, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 355}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-19T01:55:11.869Z", "request_id": "req_011CgAkpCAAhm3vW5YG6funx"}, {"type